# Power-Hybrid Battery Health & Fault Diagnosis Pipeline — Technical Documentation

Companion document to `power_hybrid_battery_diagnosis.ipynb`. This file explains the problem, the data,
the model, why it was chosen, how it's trained/calibrated, what it outputs, and how to run it in production.

---

## 1. Problem & Objective

**What it solves:** a hybrid power system (battery pack + distribution bus) needs to know, continuously and
automatically, whether it is **healthy (H)** or **unhealthy (UH)** — and ideally *which* fault it has —
using only onboard voltage/current/temperature telemetry, without waiting for a full failure.

**Why it matters:**
- **Safety** — thermal runaway precursors need to be caught in seconds, not after the fact.
- **Availability** — bus-level electrical faults (overcurrent, undervoltage) can cascade into load-shedding
  or subsystem loss if not flagged immediately.
- **Longevity/planning** — gradual battery degradation (capacity fade) needs to be tracked over time so a
  cell/pack can be scheduled for replacement or de-rated before it becomes a hard failure.
- **Constraint** — the detector must run onboard, in real time, on limited compute (no offline batch
  processing for the primary layer), which rules out heavy ML models as the *primary* method and points to
  a lightweight, causal, physics-based estimator instead.

**Objective:** classify each telemetry stream as `H` / `UH`, and (for battery faults) give a real-time state
estimate (SOC) and a physically interpretable degradation signal, while keeping compute low enough for
onboard/flight use.

