## 2. Dataset

**Source:** `power_hybrid_preview.zip` (synthetic/simulated preview dataset representing a hybrid power
system — single-cell battery telemetry + downstream bus/distribution telemetry).

**Structure:**
```
preview/
├── manifest.csv            # ground truth per test instance
├── cell/                   # cell-level (single battery cell) telemetry, 1 Hz
│   ├── normal_000.csv, normal_001.csv
│   ├── gradual_capacity_fade_000.csv, _001.csv
│   ├── accelerated_fade_000.csv, _001.csv
│   ├── thermal_runaway_precursor_000.csv, _001.csv
│   ├── bus_voltage_fault_000.csv, _001.csv          # flat placeholder (see note below)
│   └── distribution_overcurrent_000.csv, _001.csv   # flat placeholder (see note below)
└── bus/                    # bus/distribution-level telemetry, 100 Hz
    └── (same 12 instance names)
```

**Size:** 12 test instances total (6 fault types × 2 repeats). Cell files: 1,800 rows each (30 min @ 1 Hz).
Bus files: 180,000 rows each (30 min @ 100 Hz). ~2.18M rows overall.

**Labels (`manifest.csv`):**
| Column | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `instance_id` | unique test run name |
| `fault_type` | `normal`, `gradual_capacity_fade`, `accelerated_fade`, `thermal_runaway_precursor`, `bus_voltage_fault`, `distribution_overcurrent` |
| `label` | `H` (healthy) or `UH` (unhealthy) — the ground-truth target |
| `real_battery_data_used` | `True` if the cell CSV is a physics-simulated battery discharge; `False` if it's a flat placeholder (used for the two distribution-layer faults, where the fault isn't a battery phenomenon) |
| `soh_pct` | ground-truth State-of-Health percentage for that cell (100% = new; ~82% = faded) |

**Preprocessing done in the pipeline:**
- Coulomb counting: `Q(t) = -∫I dt` (Ah) from cell current, to derive an SOC(%) trace for the reference
  (healthy) cell.
- Smoothing: Savitzky–Golay filter (window 51, order 3) to build a clean reference voltage-vs-SOC curve
  from noisy raw data, and (window 21, order 3) to smooth the interpolated dQ/dV curve.
- Resampling: dQ/dV is computed on a uniform 300-point voltage grid (via linear interpolation) rather than
  raw irregular timestamps, so the derivative is well-behaved.
- Robust statistics for the bus layer: 1st/99th percentiles instead of raw min/max, since raw min/max over
  180,000 samples is a noisy extreme-value statistic even for healthy data.

**Known dataset limitation (documented, not hidden):** all cell discharges are a single, roughly constant
~1C-rate discharge. There is no HPPC pulse/relaxation test and no low-rate (≈C/20) sweep, both of which are
normally required to cleanly separate ohmic resistance from open-circuit voltage, and to get artifact-free
dQ/dV peaks. The pipeline works around this transparently (see §5, §9) rather than pretending the data is
richer than it is.

