## 5. Why This Model (vs. Alternatives)

| Option | Verdict | Reasoning |
|---|---|---|
| **ECM + EKF** (chosen, primary) | ✅ | Causal (no future data needed), low compute, runs sample-by-sample — the standard architecture behind flight/EV battery management systems. Gives a state estimate *and* an anomaly signal from the same pass. Well-understood tuning/convergence behavior, important for flight heritage. |
| Black-box ML classifier (e.g., LSTM/RandomForest on raw signals) as the *primary* onboard detector | ❌ (not primary) | Needs a training set; with only 2 instances per class this dataset can't support a generalizable model, and even with more data, a black-box model is harder to certify/validate for a safety-critical onboard role and doesn't give an interpretable physical state (SOC) the rest of the system needs anyway. |
| dQ/dV (ICA) as the *only* method | ❌ (used as secondary only) | Very sensitive to *shape* changes (great for subtle capacity fade — see §7), but needs a full, reasonably clean, ideally slow (≈C/20) charge/discharge sweep to compute — not compatible with real-time, arbitrary-duty-cycle onboard operation. |
| Simple fixed thresholds on raw V/I/T (no model) | ❌ (used only where appropriate: bus layer) | Fine for the bus layer, where faults are direct electrical excursions. Not adequate for battery-cell faults, where degradation shows up as a *pattern relative to SOC/current*, not a fixed absolute limit — an ECM/EKF is needed to separate "expected voltage sag" from "abnormal voltage sag." |

**Net design:** use the right tool per layer — ECM+EKF for cell electrochemistry (primary, onboard),
dQ/dV for the specific failure mode (subtle capacity fade) the primary method is weakest at (secondary,
ground diagnostic), and simple statistical rules for the bus layer, where a battery model would be the
wrong abstraction entirely (confirmed empirically by the dataset's own `real_battery_data_used=False` flag).

