## 7. Output / Results

Running the notebook against the preview dataset gives, per instance:

| Instance | Fault | True label | EKF residual flag | dQ/dV peak shift (V) | Final verdict | Correct? |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| normal_000/001 | normal | H | H | 0.000 | H | ✓ |
| gradual_capacity_fade_000/001 | gradual_capacity_fade | UH | H | 0.255 | UH | ✓ |
| accelerated_fade_000/001 | accelerated_fade | UH | H | 0.152 | UH | ✓ |
| thermal_runaway_precursor_000/001 | thermal_runaway_precursor | UH | UH | 0.081 | UH | ✓ |
| bus_voltage_fault_000/001 | bus_voltage_fault | UH | n/a | — | UH | ✓ |
| distribution_overcurrent_000/001 | distribution_overcurrent | UH | n/a | — | UH | ✓ |

**Overall accuracy on this 12-instance preview set: 12/12 = 100%** (after calibrating the bus detector on
robust percentile features — the initial raw-min/max version scored 11/12, missing one overcurrent case;
see §9).

**Key findings:**
- The **EKF voltage residual alone** correctly separates `normal` from `thermal_runaway_precursor`
  (residual std ≈ 0.03 V vs ≈0.014 V for healthy; max residual and `max dT/dt` both clear the healthy-based
  threshold), but **does not** reliably separate `normal` from `gradual_capacity_fade` on a single ~1C
  discharge — the voltage-trajectory effect of subtle fade is smaller than the noise floor of a single-shot
  residual.
- **dQ/dV peak-voltage shift** clearly separates all three degraded-battery cases from `normal`
  (0.15–0.26 V shift vs. 0 V for the healthy baseline itself), including the case (`gradual_capacity_fade`)
  where the EKF residual was weakest — confirming it as the right complementary check.
- **Bus-level z-score rules** cleanly separate `bus_voltage_fault` (voltage z-score ≈ −42 to −44, vs. ≈ −3
  for healthy) and, once computed on robust percentile features, `distribution_overcurrent` (current
  z-score ≈ 3.9–14.5, vs. ≈ 1.8–2.4 for healthy).

