## 9. Implementation

**Language/runtime:** Python 3, designed for Google Colab (no GPU needed — everything here is O(n) per
timestep, runs in well under a second per instance on CPU).

**Key libraries:**
- `numpy` — array math, EKF linear algebra.
- `pandas` — CSV loading, manifest handling, feature tables.
- `scipy.signal.savgol_filter` — reference-curve and dQ/dV smoothing.
- `matplotlib` — all plots (voltage/current/temperature traces, residuals, dQ/dV overlay).
- `google.colab.files` (Colab-only) — zip upload handling.

**Hardware/software requirements:** any machine that can run Python + numpy/scipy/pandas/matplotlib;
no GPU, no deep learning framework. Runs comfortably in the free Colab tier.

**Key code components (all in `power_hybrid_battery_diagnosis.ipynb`):**
- `run_ekf(instance_id, Qmax_ref)` — the EKF loop described in §4.2/§8A.
- `Vref_of_soc(soc)` — interpolation lookup for the empirical reference curve.
- `differential_capacity(instance_id, n_grid=300)` — the dQ/dV computation described in §4.3.
- Bus feature/threshold block — the rule-based detector described in §4.4.
- Final fusion + accuracy block — described in §4.5 / §7.

**Notable implementation fix applied during validation:** the first version of the bus detector used raw
`min()`/`max()` over the 180,000-sample bus files as its z-score input; because raw extremes are noisy even
for healthy data, this misclassified one `distribution_overcurrent` instance. Switching to 1st/99th
percentile features (a more robust "sustained excursion" statistic) fixed this and brought accuracy to
12/12 — documented here as an example of why every stage was executed end-to-end and checked against ground
truth rather than assumed correct from the design alone.

